      {
        "id": "p10-q15-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$2<x<4$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$5<x^2+1<17$.",
          "worked": "On $2<x<4$, $x$ is positive, so squaring preserves the order.\n$$\\begin{align*}4&<x^2<16\\\\5&<x^2+1<17\\end{align*}$$\nNeither endpoint is included.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-q15-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$-1<x\\le3$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1\\le x^2+1\\le10$.\n\nThe minimum occurs at the vertex $x=0$; the maximum occurs at $x=3$.",
          "worked": "The graph of $y=x^2+1$ has its minimum at $x=0$, which lies in the given interval.\n$$\\begin{align*}y(0)&=0^2+1=1\\\\y(3)&=3^2+1=10\\end{align*}$$\nOn $-1<x<0$, $1<y<2$; on $0\\le x\\le3$, $1\\le y\\le10$. Hence $1\\le x^2+1\\le10$, with both bounds attained.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 31
      }
    ]
  },
